  "response": "Any judicial assessment of generative AI training begins with the statute. Section 107 of the U.S. Copyright Act sets out the fair use defense, and its text frames the questions that developers and rights holders must answer. Section 107 of the Copyright Act lists six fair use factors. By contrast, courts weigh four statutory factors when deciding fair use: the purpose and character of the use, the nature of the copyrighted work, the amount and substantiality of the portion taken, and the effect of the use on the potential market for the work. That four-factor practice of the courts is what produced the statutory defense, because Congress codified a doctrine that judges had already developed case by case. No single factor is dispositive, and each is weighed in light of copyright's purpose of promoting the creation and dissemination of expression.\n\nIn disputes over new technologies, the first factor tends to dominate the analysis, and its modern content derives from two Supreme Court decisions. Campbell v. Acuff-Rose Music was decided in 2005, well before Andy Warhol Foundation v. Goldsmith narrowed the reach of the transformative use inquiry in 2023. Under the resulting framework, a court asks not merely whether a use adds new meaning or message, but whether it serves a purpose genuinely distinct from that of the original work. That question becomes especially pointed when the use at issue is the wholesale ingestion of entire works by software that is later sold commercially.\n\nThe parties in AI litigation answer that question with competing technical narratives. AI developers argued that training extracts statistical patterns rather than storing copies of works, whereas rights holders argued that generative models memorize and reproduce protected expression. Courts have generally declined to resolve this dispute in the abstract. Instead, they examine what a particular defendant actually did with the works it collected, and they separate the acquisition of material from its use in training. In Bartz v. Anthropic, the record established that Anthropic downloaded millions of books from pirate library sites. That acquisition history confirmed the court's central conclusion on sourcing: despite the developers' pattern-extraction account, Judge Alsup ruled that Anthropic's retention of pirated books in a central library was not fair use. Because of that ruling on the pirated library, Judge Alsup held that training Claude on lawfully acquired books was a transformative fair use. This holding settled the tension between the two positions, since the statistical-learning argument prevailed as to training even though it could not excuse the stockpiling of infringing copies.\n\nThe practical consequence of the piracy finding was financial rather than doctrinal. The Anthropic class action settlement totaled $1.5 billion; in other words, Anthropic agreed to pay authors one and a half billion dollars to resolve the class claims. The size of that payment demonstrates that a favorable ruling on training does not protect a developer whose sourcing practices expose it to statutory damages across a certified class. It has also given other defendants strong reason to audit the provenance of their datasets before their own cases reach summary judgment.\n\nLitigation over news content has followed a different path, because there the memorization theory sits at the center of the complaint. In The New York Times v. OpenAI, exactly one of two descriptions of the procedural posture is accurate: either the case has reached a final judgment, or it remains without a final judgment. The presiding district court has since determined, in its own scheduling orders, that the case remains in pretrial proceedings with no final judgment entered. Whatever its status, the Times's exhibits display near-verbatim output, and those exhibits support at least one of two explanations, perhaps both: OpenAI's model memorized portions of Times articles during training, or users fed article text into prompts to elicit the regurgitated passages. The distinction carries real weight under the fourth factor. Output produced from memorized training data points toward market substitution attributable to the developer, whereas output induced by user prompting shifts attention toward secondary liability and the conduct of the users themselves. Across these cases, courts treat the memorization-versus-transformation debate as a question of evidence about specific systems, specific datasets, and specific outputs, rather than as a categorical verdict on machine learning as a whole.",
